\section*{Limitations}

The semantic layer introduces cross-field consistency (CF) violations (up to 12.02\%) under zero-shot prompting, a systematic consequence of per-relation independent enforcement that remains an open challenge; this rate falls to $\leq$1.18\% once the base model is LoRA fine-tuned (Table~\ref{tab:composability}).
% CF rates: Qwen FT S1 1.18%; Llama FT S1 0.46%
A pilot variant that triggers grammar-first fallback on entity-type re-assignments reduces CF to 0.04\% but re-routes the same mass into a 13.85\,pp DR increase (Appendix~\ref{app:discussion}), indicating that genuine CF resolution requires global entity-level type consensus rather than local intervention.
Entity F1 impact is model-dependent under zero-shot ($+$1.22\,pp for Qwen vs.\ $-$0.64\,pp for Llama on DocRED), partially confounded by increased parse failures under constraints (Llama: 9.1\% vs.\ 6.8\%); under fine-tuning, entity F1 is unchanged for both models on SciERC.
Results cover two 8B-class models on two datasets, in both zero-shot and LoRA fine-tuned settings on SciERC; DocRED fine-tuning is not reported (the Qwen LoRA run is still in progress at submission time).
Validation at larger scales (e.g.\ 70B-class models) and on non-English ontologies remains to be explored.

% === Ethics Statement ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available benchmark datasets (SciERC and DocRED) with no personally identifiable information.
No human subjects were involved in the experiments.
The constrained decoding method operates on model outputs and does not introduce new privacy or safety risks beyond those inherent in the base language models.

